WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.246
In this part we meet two more types.

2
00:00:03.246 --> 00:00:12.354
In the first, multiplying y and all its derivatives by the same number leaves the equation unchanged.

3
00:00:12.354 --> 00:00:19.117
In the second, we give x and y different weights so that all terms balance.

4
00:00:19.117 --> 00:00:22.454
In both cases the order drops by one.

5
00:00:22.704 --> 00:00:33.326
If the equation is homogeneous in y, y prime and y double prime, substitute y prime equals y times z.

6
00:00:33.326 --> 00:00:39.425
Then y double prime equals y times z prime plus z squared.

7
00:00:39.425 --> 00:00:46.787
After cancelling the power of y, a first-order equation for z remains.

8
00:00:46.787 --> 00:00:52.886
Note: this is yet another meaning of the word homogeneous.

9
00:00:53.136 --> 00:01:01.287
Example: x y y double prime minus x times y prime squared equals y y prime.

10
00:01:01.287 --> 00:01:04.656
Every term has degree two in y.

11
00:01:04.656 --> 00:01:12.264
After the substitution we cancel y squared and get x z prime equals z.

12
00:01:12.264 --> 00:01:14.438
So z equals C one x.

13
00:01:14.688 --> 00:01:18.220
Now y prime over y equals C one x.

14
00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:25.907
Integrating, y equals C two times e to the power one half C one x squared.

15
00:01:25.907 --> 00:01:32.451
With C two equal to zero, the zero function is also a solution.

16
00:01:32.451 --> 00:01:37.022
The result is easy to check by substitution.

17
00:01:37.272 --> 00:01:41.119
Now give x weight one and y weight m.

18
00:01:41.119 --> 00:01:48.084
Then y prime has weight m minus one and y double prime m minus two.

19
00:01:48.084 --> 00:01:58.273
For x cubed y double prime equals the square of x y prime minus y, we get m plus one equals two m,

20
00:01:58.273 --> 00:01:59.937
so m equals one.

21
00:01:59.937 --> 00:02:07.838
The substitution x equals e to the t, y equals e to the t times u removes t.

22
00:02:08.088 --> 00:02:11.944
The last equation contains neither t nor u,

23
00:02:11.944 --> 00:02:17.951
so put q equal to the derivative of u and get a separable equation.

24
00:02:17.951 --> 00:02:26.200
Answer: y equals x ln x minus x ln of the absolute value of one minus C one x, plus C two x.

25
00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:34.718
Also, the case q equals zero gives the solutions y equals C x, which must be listed separately.

26
00:02:34.968 --> 00:02:45.310
The graph shows the curves y equals e to the one half C one x squared from the first example for three values of C one.

27
00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:54.782
For positive C one the curve grows, for negative it decays like a bell, and for zero it is a horizontal line.

28
00:02:55.032 --> 00:03:06.092
Check yourself: which substitution fits an equation homogeneous in y, y prime and y double prime?

29
00:03:06.092 --> 00:03:08.030
Choose an answer.
